<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Wazir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Banu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">Wazir</a> were a Muslim family of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> who rose to power when Almoravid rule broke down in the 1140s. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidray-ibn-wazir">Sidray ibn Wazir</a>, they ruled Beja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a> in present-day Portugal, and in 1145 members of the family took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, forming one of the short-lived second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms. They soon came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>.</p></div>
